54,3,1,talk,Misaki,,I lean toward D. Having an engineering team present during the rollout is the only way to ensure our depot supervisors actually adopt the system and don't revert to their own routines.,"{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
55,3,1,talk,Kenji,,I like D too! Having the engineers right there means they can see our old scanners and the chaos of the yard for themselves. It's way better than just hoping it works!,"{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
56,3,1,talk,Toshio,,"I cannot support Candidate A. Its modular, independently licensed structure is exactly the kind of financial trap I discovered, where separate add-ons lead to unchecked, climbing operating costs.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
57,3,1,talk,Yumi,,"I'm a bit worried about C because it uses a partner firm for migration and training. After that outage last winter, I really want a vendor who takes full responsibility instead of passing the buck!","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
58,3,1,talk,Benjamin,,"I share Yumi's apprehension regarding Candidate C. Entrusting the data migration to a partner firm is a recipe for the same ""sloppy lift"" and lingering errors we suffered in 2018. It is far too risky.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Yumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
59,3,2,talk,Misaki,,"@Toshio, I agree that A is a risk. Between B and D, D's on-site presence is more valuable given our administrator shortage. We cannot afford a ""fixed configuration"" that doesn't account for our actual staffing gaps.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
60,3,2,talk,Kenji,,"@Misaki, you're right! B's ""fixed configuration"" sounds scary. If it doesn't fit our weird yard rules or our old gear, we'll just end up back with those spreadsheets again! D feels much safer.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Misaki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,2,talk,Toshio,,"@Misaki, @Kenji, while on-site support is beneficial, we must consider the long-term cost. Candidate D's on-premise deployment often hides maintenance costs that shift from capital to operating budgets, which the board rarely scrutinizes.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Misaki"",""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,2,talk,Yumi,,"@Toshio, that's a scary thought! But if we go with B, will the regional team be as helpful as the on-site engineers in D? I just want to make sure someone is truly in charge when we need them!","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@Toshio, your point on hidden maintenance is valid, yet B's ""fixed configuration"" is a different kind of trap. It risks becoming a system that ages in place, unable to adapt to the rule changes I discovered.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,3,talk,Misaki,,"@Benjamin, exactly. If the system is too rigid, the burden of making it work falls on our already stretched administrators. D's engineering team can help bridge that gap during the critical transition period.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,3,talk,Kenji,,"@Toshio, I get the money part, but if the system doesn't work in the yard, it's a waste of money anyway! D is the only one that feels like they'll actually get their hands dirty with us.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,3,talk,Toshio,,"@Kenji, I do not dispute the need for functionality. However, Candidate B offers a hosted suite, which typically implies a more bundled and predictable cost structure than on-premise maintenance. It avoids the fragmented licensing of A and the partner-dependency of C.","{""words"":41,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,3,talk,Yumi,,"@Toshio, I see your point about the costs! But I'm still worried about the ""fixed configuration"" in B. If it doesn't match our business, we'll just be stressed again! D feels like a more supportive friend.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,3,talk,Benjamin,,"@Toshio, while B is fiscally predictable, it lacks the agility to survive our volatile warehouse rules. D's on-site engineering team provides the necessary safeguard against the ""external repair"" cycle I find so distasteful. I vote for D.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,4,talk,Misaki,,"I agree with Benjamin. Given our staffing crisis and the tendency of depot crews to ignore standard training, the on-site support of Candidate D is essential. I vote for D.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,4,talk,Kenji,,I'm with everyone! D is the only one that feels like it can survive our peak-season rush and our old gear. I'm voting for D!,"{""words"":25,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,4,talk,Toshio,,"While I remain cautious about the long-term maintenance costs of on-premise software, the consensus on operational risk is clear. Candidate B is too rigid, and A and C are unacceptable. I vote for D.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,4,talk,Yumi,,"I agree! Even if the costs are a bit tricky, having the engineers right here with us makes me feel so much safer. I'm voting for D too!","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,4,talk,Benjamin,,It appears we have reached a most harmonious conclusion. Candidate D provides the necessary operational safeguards and direct accountability we require. I concur with the group; my vote is for D.,"{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
